# Wasabi Base Conventions

## Tech Stack
- Backend is Java (bytecode targets Java 8; build and run on JDK 17+) with Maven multi-module builds (`pom.xml` root + `modules/*/pom.xml`).
- JDBC connection pooling uses HikariCP (`modules/database`).
- DI and service wiring use Guice and Autumn service modules.
- HTTP APIs use JAX-RS annotations (`@Path`, `@GET`, `@POST`) and Swagger annotations.
- Persistence targets MySQL and Cassandra (DataStax); many flows are repository-driven.
- Legacy frontend in `modules/ui` is AngularJS 1.x + Grunt + Karma/Jasmine.
- New frontend in `modules/ui-react` is React 18 + TypeScript + Vite + React Router + React Query + Tailwind CSS (with shadcn/ui-style components).

## Repository Structure
- Keep backend code in `modules/<domain>/src/main/java/com/intuit/wasabi/...`.
- Keep backend tests in `modules/<domain>/src/test/java/...` (or `integration-test/test/java` where already used).
- Keep UI app code under `modules/ui/app/`:
  - `scripts/controllers/*Ctrl.js`
  - `scripts/services/*Factory.js`
  - `scripts/directives/*.js`
  - `views/*.html`
- Keep React UI code under `modules/ui-react/src/`:
  - `pages/*.tsx` route-level screens
  - `components/layout/*.tsx` app chrome/layout
  - `components/ui/*.tsx` reusable primitive UI components
  - `api/*.ts` HTTP client wrappers
  - `types/*.ts` shared frontend types
  - `lib/*.ts` shared utilities
- Treat `contrib/` as examples/integrations; avoid coupling core backend logic to it.

## Naming Conventions
- Java packages stay under `com.intuit.wasabi.<module>`.
- Java classes use PascalCase; prefer role-revealing suffixes already present:
  - API resources: `*Resource`
  - Guice modules: `*Module`
  - repository implementations: `*Repository`, `Cassandra*Repository`
  - exceptions: `*Exception`
  - tests: unit `*Test`, integration `*IT`
- AngularJS controllers use `*Ctrl` names and register with `.controller('NameCtrl', ...)`.
- AngularJS service factories use `*Factory` naming and `.factory('NameFactory', ...)`.
- React components/pages use PascalCase filenames and named exports where practical (`Login`, `Experiments`, `Layout`).
- Use path aliases from `modules/ui-react/components.json` (`@/components`, `@/lib`, `@/api`, `@/types`) instead of deep relative imports.

## Coding Patterns to Follow
- Prefer constructor injection (`@Inject`) for backend services/components.
- Keep API methods thin: authorize, parse/validate inputs, delegate to domain services, return response.
- Preserve explicit try/catch logging pattern around API endpoints when touching existing resources.
- Reuse existing domain value types (`Experiment.ID`, `Application.Name`, `Context`, etc.) rather than raw strings.
- Prefer existing helpers over ad-hoc duplication (Java services and UI utility/factory patterns).
- Maintain behavior first in legacy code; avoid broad refactors unless requested.
- For React data fetching, follow existing React Query + axios client pattern (`apiClient` + `useQuery`/`refetch`) instead of manual duplicated fetch logic.
- Keep auth/token access in one place (`apiClient` interceptor + route guards) and avoid scattering storage/headers logic.

## Style and Safety
- Match file-local formatting and idioms; do not force modern rewrites in legacy AngularJS or Java files.
- Keep public API behavior and endpoint contracts backward compatible unless explicitly asked otherwise.
- Add or update tests alongside behavior changes in the corresponding module.
- For UI JavaScript in `modules/ui`, preserve 2-space indentation and existing AngularJS style.
- For `modules/ui-react`, prefer TypeScript strictness, functional components/hooks, and existing ESLint rules (`eslint.config.js`).
